    "era": "Baroque to Modern",
    "sum": "Alla prima completes an oil painting in a single wet session, laying fresh color into still-wet paint without underpainting or glazes. Frans Hals' flicked portraits and Sargent's dashing likenesses are its high points.",
    "desc": "Working from thin darks to thick lights before anything dries, the painter must judge color, value and edge in one pass, which keeps hues clean and edges lively - mixing on the canvas itself rather than on the palette. Speed produces its signature look: unblended juxtaposed strokes, wet-into-wet smears where colors meet, and a surface that records a decisive hour of looking. Hals built laughing faces from slash-like strokes, the 1841 collapsible tin tube made the method portable, and Sargent or Anders Zorn turned it into society-portrait virtuosity; today it is the default language of oil sketching and portrait demonstrations.",
