    "sum": "Plein air painting means executing the picture outdoors, in front of the motif, chasing changing light with portable gear. It is the founding discipline of the Barbizon School and Impressionism, made possible by the 1841 paint tube.",
    "desc": "The painter sets up a box easel and limited palette directly before the subject, blocking in big value masses quickly because cloud and sun alter the scene within an hour or two; wet-into-wet handling and pre-tinted panels keep pace with the light. Corot's campagna oil studies, the Barbizon painters' Fontainebleau woods, Monet's serial haystacks and Rouen facades, and Winslow Homer's seaside turned direct observation into modernism's evidence base. The finish is characteristically fresh and generalized - accurate relationships of hue and temperature matter more than detail - and contemporary plein air events have revived the practice worldwide.",
