    "p": "c. 1595-1650",
    "era": "Baroque",
    "sum": "Tenebrism stages figures in a darkness that dominates the picture, pierced by theatrical spotlights acting like stage lighting on a bare black set. Caravaggio's Calling of Saint Matthew is the type specimen.",
    "desc": "Unlike mere strong shading, tenebrism makes darkness a compositional subject: forms are isolated in near-black fields and riveted by a single hot, often raking light that snags on hands, steel and faces. The drama carries Counter-Reformation content - conversion, martyrdom, revelation - by making the inexplicable light itself an actor, as in The Calling of Saint Matthew where the divine ray follows Christ's pointed hand. Artemisia Gentileschi, Ribera and the Utrecht Caravaggisti exported the idiom; Honthorst pushed it to single-candle night scenes, and its lighting logic still governs film noir and cinematic key-light practice.",
    "col": [{"h": "#0D0B08", "n": "Void black"}, {"h": "#1E1710", "n": "Near-black brown"}, {"h": "#C99A5B", "n": "Spotlit flesh"}, {"h": "#D9A84E", "n": "Candle gold"}, {"h": "#6E1D24", "n": "Deep crimson"}, {"h": "#EFE6D0", "n": "Highlight white"}],
