    "era": "Renaissance to Baroque",
    "sum": "Chiaroscuro is the systematic modeling of form through contrasts of light and shade, giving painted figures the solidity of sculpture. It spans Leonardo's soft volumes, Rembrandt's glowing interiors and the chiaroscuro woodcut print.",
    "desc": "The painter plans a light logic - one dominant source, graded halftones, reflected lights in the shadows - so every form turns convincingly in space, from Leonardo's Virgin of the Rocks to Rembrandt's The Night Watch, where light picks a crowd into a narrative. Georges de La Tour compressed it into candlelit geometry, while in printmaking the chiaroscuro woodcut (Ugo da Carpi, Goltzius) achieved tonal effects with two or three inked blocks. As a principle rather than a single recipe, chiaroscuro underlies academic value study, cinematic lighting design and photographic low-key work.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F4EFE4", "n": "Lit white"}, {"h": "#C9B08A", "n": "Golden halftone"}, {"h": "#7A6248", "n": "Mid umber"}, {"h": "#46372A", "n": "Reflected shadow"}, {"h": "#20180F", "n": "Core dark"}],
