    "p": "Antiquity to present; peak 1650-1900",
    "era": "Baroque to Victorian",
    "sum": "Trompe-l'œil paints with such verisimilitude and true scale that flat objects appear physically present - a letter pinned to a board, a boy escaping a frame. Its test is the outstretched hand of the fooled viewer.",
    "desc": "Success requires actual-size objects, razor-sharp focus, exact cast shadows aligned to the room's real light, and shallow or zero perspective depth - unlike grand illusionistic ceilings, which use forced perspective from a fixed station point. Seventeenth-century Dutch painters like Samuel van Hoogstraten pinned letters, scissors and razors onto pale boards; Andrea Pozzo's Sant'Ignazio ceiling extended the idea into architecture (quadratura); Pere Borrell del Caso's Escaping Criticism shows a boy climbing out of his frame, and William Harnett fooled American jury-goers with painted dollar bills. The genre thrives today in street murals and decorative panels.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F2EEE6", "n": "Faux marble white"}, {"h": "#C7C2B4", "n": "Cool board gray"}, {"h": "#8A8577", "n": "Shadow gray"}, {"h": "#6B4F35", "n": "Wood tone"}, {"h": "#A6B6C8", "n": "Ceiling sky"}, {"h": "#57534A", "n": "Cast shadow"}],
