    "era": "Modern",
    "sum": "Gouache is opaque watercolor - pigment loaded with chalk or white - that dries fast to flat, velvety, light-reflecting films. It powered mid-century posters, animation backgrounds and Matisse's cut-outs.",
    "desc": "Because added white gives opacity, gouache covers mistakes, sits above underlying layers and blocks in solid shape without needing the white of paper to glow, which makes it ideal for design: hard edges, uniform fields, and reworkable passages. It was the studio workhorse of advertising art, theater design and film animation backgrounds, and Cassandre-style posters and mid-century children's books live on its matte flatness. Matisse cut his late compositions from paper he had painted in gouache (papiers decoupes), and contemporary flat-design illustration revives the look digitally. Its chalky matte body is instantly recognizable against glossy oils or transparent washes.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F6F2E7", "n": "Paper white"}, {"h": "#E8613C", "n": "Tomato red"}, {"h": "#F2C94C", "n": "Mustard yellow"}, {"h": "#4E7D7B", "n": "Muted teal"}, {"h": "#E9B44C", "n": "Naples gold"}, {"h": "#2B2B2B", "n": "Design ink"}],
