    "era": "Imperial China",
    "sum": "Gongbi is Chinese meticulous brush painting: razor-fine ink outlines filled with graded mineral-pigment washes on silk. Emperor Huizong's parakeets and Qiu Ying's handscrolls exemplify its patient, jeweled precision.",
    "desc": "The painter first draws baimiao - hairline ink contours of a bird's feather rows or a flower's veins - with an upright, pressure-controlled brush, then floods even washes of malachite, azurite, cinnabar or gamboge between the lines, building dozens of layers over silk sized with alum. The result is exact to the point of taxonomy: Song bird-and-flower albums record species, plumage and petal count, yet stay lyrical through idealized spacing and breathing negative space. Huizong's Five-Colored Parakeet (1112) carries his瘦金-style inscription, Ming master Qiu Ying perfected the manner, and Jesuit court painter Giuseppe Castiglione (Lang Shining) fused it with European shading in the 18th century.",
