    "era": "Medieval East Asia",
    "sum": "Ink wash painting builds entire images from black sumi ink's five tonalities, using water-loaded brushwork on absorbent paper. Sesshu's splashed-ink landscapes and Tōhaku's pine screens distill the world into breath and stroke.",
    "desc": "Grinding an ink stick on a wet stone yields a ink whose dilution ladder - from dry burnt-black to pale mist - is called the five colors; the painter must place each stroke once, because absorbent paper forgives nothing. Composition privileges emptiness: a few angled branches, graded mountain masses dissolving into unworked paper, and the calligraphic body mechanics shared with writing. Zen monks carried the mode from Song China to Japan, where Sesshu Toyo's haboku (splashed-ink) landscapes and Hasegawa Tohaku's Pine Trees screens became national icons; Xu Beihong's galloping horses and Qi Baishi's shrimp show its 20th-century life.",
