    "p": "1884-1910",
    "era": "Late 19th century",
    "sum": "Divisionism applies pigment in small separated strokes of pure color intended to blend optically in the eye rather than on the palette, systematized from contemporary color science by Seurat and Signac.",
    "desc": "Grounded in Chevreul's simultaneous-contrast law and Ogden Rood's Modern Chromatics, the divisionist lays complementary and adjacent hues side by side - dot by measured dot in Seurat's pointille, comma-like strokes in Signac - so additive mixing in the retina yields luminosity impossible from stirred mud. A Sunday on La Grande Jatte took two years of studies and repainting to calibrate; Signac's Mediterranean sails, Henri-Edmond Cross's shimmering coastlines and Italian divisionists like Pellizza da Volpedo carried the method forward. Though often conflated with Pointillism (its dot-based branch), divisionism names the optical-mixing principle, with contrasts of hue, tone and line mapped almost musically.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F2E14C", "n": "Optical yellow"}, {"h": "#7FB2E5", "n": "Aerial blue"}, {"h": "#E86A4E", "n": "Complementary orange"}, {"h": "#7A4E8C", "n": "Cobalt violet"}, {"h": "#4C8C5A", "n": "Emerald green"}, {"h": "#F5E9D0", "n": "Sunlit ground"}],
    "m": ["Zinc and lead whites", "Pure spectrum pigments", "Small fine brushes", "Color wheel and Rood's Modern Chromatics", "Pre-mixed value strings"],
    "tx": ["Woven dot-fabric surface", "Shimmering optical vibration", "Uniform granular grain"],
