    "p": "c. 1480 to present",
    "era": "Renaissance to Contemporary",
    "sum": "Anamorphosis distorts an image so it resolves only from one vantage point or in a curved mirror - most famously Holbein's stretched skull in The Ambassadors. It weaponizes perspective against casual seeing.",
    "desc": "The maker computes a projection grid - oblique, catoptric (mirror), or mathematical - then paints the subject through it, so from most angles the canvas shows abstract smear while the chosen viewpoint reconstructs the form. Holbein's Ambassadors (1533) floats a memento mori skull across its floor; Erhard Schön printed secret portraits for Reformation pamphlets; 17th-century cylinder anamorphoses needed polished cones to unscramble courtly puzzles. Modern heirs include Julian Beever and Edgar Mueller's pavement illusions, which realign a whole street's sidewalk perspective for a single camera position.",
    "col": [{"h": "#E8E2D2", "n": "Aged paper"}, {"h": "#7A6248", "n": "Brown wash"}, {"h": "#3B352C", "n": "Sepia ink"}, {"h": "#C9B88F", "n": "Ochre ground"}, {"h": "#9A9284", "n": "Smear gray"}],
    "m": ["Perspective grids and vantage templates", "Polished cylinder mirror (catoptric)", "Chalk and pastel for pavement work", "Ink and wash", "Fixed viewpoint scaffold"],
    "tx": ["Stretched smear at oblique angles", "Snapping clarity at the true viewpoint", "Grid-derived distortion"],
    "obj": ["Double-view portraits", "Secret courtly images", "Cylinder-mirror puzzles", "3D pavement art", "Signage and branding stunts"],
