    "p": "c. 1920s to present",
    "era": "Modern",
    "sum": "Blind contour drawing traces a subject's edges while watching only the subject, never the paper, training eye-hand sync and producing delightfully distorted, wiry lines. Kimon Nicolaides institutionalized it in The Natural Way to Draw.",
    "desc": "The artist fixes gaze on the model or object and moves the pen as though the point were touching the observed contour, climbing every bump at the speed of sight without lifting or checking; because the hand receives no visual correction, the result is a trembling, headless, often hilarious anatomy whose distortions record duration and attention rather than likeness. Nicolaides' 1941 textbook made it the first exercise of modern drawing pedagogy, Betty Edwards' Drawing on the Right Side of the Brain carried it to millions, and Egon Schiele-adjacent and contemporary illustrators deliberately exhibit blind-contour distortion as expressive style. It is a warm-up, a meditation, and an aesthetic in its own right.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F4EFE2", "n": "Sketch paper"}, {"h": "#14140F", "n": "Fountain ink"}, {"h": "#2B4A9B", "n": "Ballpoint blue"}, {"h": "#4A4A48", "n": "Graphite gray"}],
