    "p": "c. 1907 to present",
    "era": "Modern",
    "sum": "One-line drawing reduces a subject to a single unbroken pencil or brush line that never lifts from the paper, finding the minimum path that still says horse, camel or face. Picasso's animal lithographs are the touchstones.",
    "desc": "Practitioners plan an economy of route - every edge does double duty as contour and feature - then commit in one confident pass, since erasure would betray the form; the best examples leave elegant overlaps where the path crosses itself, reading as both drawing and diagram. Picasso's late animal sketches (The Dog, the camel) distilled volumes into a dozen centimeters of continuous ink, Jean Cocteau's profile drawings decorated books and walls, and Alexander Calder translated the same economy into wire portraits that hang as line in space. The style's modern descendants span logos, tattoos and social-media illustration, all trading on its instant, self-assured elegance.",
    "col": [{"h": "#F6F1E4", "n": "Ivory paper"}, {"h": "#1A1A17", "n": "Sable ink"}, {"h": "#26489C", "n": "Ballpoint blue"}, {"h": "#C43A31", "n": "Fineliner red"}],
