    "sum": "Stippling builds tone entirely from dots whose density alone creates value, producing soft, granular, museum-grade shading without any line. Bartolozzi's stipple engravings and Seurat's conté drawings anchor the tradition.",
    "desc": "The artist probes thousands of individual touches - pen points, engraving needles or even curved rocker tools in printmaking - packing them tight for shadow and scattering them into paper-white breath for light; because no two dots connect, edges stay fuzzy and textures feel organic, which made the method beloved for botanical and scientific plates where hair, pollen and skin need gentle gradation. Francesco Bartolozzi's 18th-century stipple engravings spread chalky rouge-toned portraits across Europe, the engravers of Gustave Dore's Bible plates used white-line stipple to render smoke and night, and Seurat's conté crayon drawings are effectively giant stipple fields of luminous black.",
