    "sum": "Cross-hatching shades forms with layers of parallel lines set at crossing angles, letting line spacing and layer count encode value. It is the native language of engraving, etching and classical pen drawing.",
    "desc": "A first pass of parallel strokes sets the lightest tone; successive passes turn 30, 45, then 90 degrees across it, each added layer darkening the mesh until the deepest shadows almost weld into black, while following the form's direction makes surfaces feel turned in space. Durer's Melencolia I wrings every value from an engraving burin's disciplined hatch, Rembrandt's etchings loosen the network into expressive scribble-hatch, and Goya's Los Caprichos show how hatched chaos can carry satire. Because printing presses for centuries reproduced only black ink, hatching became the universal tone system of illustration, manga screentone logic and topographic shading all descend from it.",
