    "era": "Edo Japan",
    "sum": "Gyotaku is Japanese fish printing: sumi ink is brushed onto a real fish, and washi paper is rubbed against it to lift an exact monochrome record. Anglers used it as trophy evidence; museums hang it as art.",
    "desc": "The angler pins a fresh catch on a board, paints it with sumi in graded sweeps that honor scale and fin structure, then lays damp washi over the body and pats from center outward so every scale, gill plate and fin ray registers; peeling the sheet away leaves a mirrored, breathing record signed and often annotated with place, weight and date. Direct method (chokusetsu-ho) uses the cleaned fish itself, indirect method (kansetsu-ho) prints through a paper-thin mold, and the haiku poet Yosa Buson made early examples. Record prints (tairyo-batsu) still hang in fishing households, while contemporary artists extend the practice to crabs, leaves andInstallation-scale marine portraits.",
